#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2, \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2)$, and $(-2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/795c7197b03931fd6387f1c32465d057.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -3xy^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = y+\\cos x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-3y^{2} + 1)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-y}^{y}(-3y^{2} + 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)[x]_{-y}^{y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)(y - (-y))dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2ydy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-6y^{3} + 2y)dy \\\\\n&= [- 6 \\cdot \\frac{y^{4}}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{3y^{4}}{2} + y^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (-24 + 4) - 0 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3)$, and $(1,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (-2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/558dcb377eb7f6945abd065d2bcdb9e0.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 7x^2y^6 +x^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) =-2xy^7+4x^2y\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -14xy^{6} + 4x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}) + (-14xy^{6} + 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{-3}^{0}7x^{2}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x, \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1)$, and $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/16b1ce554d0410d587294f9657e5e669.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-1, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-1 + 2x)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}(2x - 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}(x^{2} - x)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{2}dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2^{2} - 2)dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}2dy \\\\ = 2y\\end{vmatrix}_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(1) - 2(-1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ba9128-17dd-54d7-abc8-d292906d45b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168632} {:answer/id #uuid "ff00d4ca-0557-57cd-a01a-aa8a298ad0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168633} {:answer/id #uuid "398d552a-b5ab-5d0e-994a-2c5e945b88ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168634} {:answer/id #uuid "4392582e-f5b3-5716-b120-796cb8c58f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168635} {:answer/id #uuid "79803fbb-50fa-57d9-a270-e130b7d932e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff00d4ca-0557-57cd-a01a-aa8a298ad0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168633} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a46733a-cc9a-5e70-9bb6-b8c93a8badfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168631}] :question/id #uuid "e4e547a4-3c18-52fb-b4b9-408da110d4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126820" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y) = ⟨e^{y} + 2x^{2}y, e^{x} + 2xy^{2}⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 1)$, and $(-1, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958de1d6-be32-553d-8d66-2d59c0ddd0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-1}^{2}x(2y - 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168650} {:answer/id #uuid "993eec8c-adb1-5f3b-a5a4-9bf8186d8107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} + 5y)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168651} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7b9296-4fd2-5fcd-98c6-37a497b7f578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} - 5y)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168652} {:answer/id #uuid "c72058f2-c38d-5413-a9be-48c7bb164a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168653} {:answer/id #uuid "46e22be8-54a1-527b-a8ee-a4d73f417264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-1}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c72058f2-c38d-5413-a9be-48c7bb164a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168653} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3a1312-b415-507d-afe0-200629efab71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168649}] :question/id #uuid "bab01488-68e9-5dde-